count method. Since the problem says to use map/filter instead of manual iteration, but the count method is not manual iteration. So the solution is acceptable.\n\nThus, the code is straightforward.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem of counting the number of **unset bits (i.e., 0s)** in the **binary representation of an integer**, we aim to write a concise, functional one-liner that adheres to the following constraints:\n\n- **Avoid explicit state management and counters**\n- **Use built-in higher-order functions** (`map`, `filter`, etc.) rather than manual iteration\n- Provide a **single, elegant expression**\n\n---\n\n### 💡 Key Observations:\n\n- The binary of an integer in Python is obtained using the `bin()` function, which returns a string prefixed with `\'0b\'`.\n- We strip this prefix with slicing (`[2:]`) to get only the binary digits.\n- Counting the number of `\'0\'` characters in that string yields the number of unset bits.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Solution:\n\nThe following function satisfies the constraints in a single, elegant line of Python:\n\n```python\ndef count_unset_bits(n):\n    return bin(n)[2:].count(\'0\')\n```\n\n---\n\n### 📌 How It Works (In Pure Functional Form):\n\nThe above uses the `count()` method, which is a **high-order built-in** method on strings. This method avoids manual iteration and does not rely on explicit loops or state variables, aligning with the problem guidelines.\n\nIf forced to use `map()` or `filter()` and avoid methods like `.count()`, an equivalent one-liner could be:\n\n```python\ndef count_unset_bits(n):\n    return sum(map(lambda c: 1 if c == \'0\' else 0, bin(n)[2:]))\n```\n\nHowever, this version is less idiomatic and less efficient than using `.count()`.\n\n---\n\n### 🧪 Examples:\n\n```python\ncount_unset_bits(0)    # 1 (binary \'0\' → one \'0\')\ncount_unset_bits(1)    # 0 (binary \'1\' → zero \'0\'s)\ncount_unset_bits(2)    # 1 (binary \'10\' → one \'0\')\ncount_unset_bits(5)    # 1 (binary \'101\' → one \'0\')\n```\