ts[i+1:j] + [digits[i]] + digits[j+1:]\n\nWait, but that's complicated. Alternatively, create a new list by copying, then swapping.\n\nBut perhaps using list comprehensions to make this easier.\n\nAlternatively, in Python, list comprehensions can be used to create new lists, but for swapping, perhaps a list comprehension is not necessary. However, the company rules require that the most Pythonic solution uses list comprehensions.\n\nBut perhaps the algorithm can be adapted to use list comprehensions where possible.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the code is acceptable as is, as the side effects are local to the function and not affecting external variables. But the company's rules say to avoid side effects. So, perhaps modifying the digits list in place is considered a side effect. Therefore, we should avoid that.\n\nSo, maybe the code can be rewritten as follows:\n\nInstead of modifying the digits list in place, create new lists each time.\n\nFor example, after finding i and j:\n\ndigits = list(str(n))\n\ni = ... found as before.\n\nj = ... found as before.\n\nThen, digits = digits[:i] + [digits[j]] + digits[i+1:j] + [digits[i]] + digits[j+1:]\n\nThen, reverse the part after i.\n\ndigits = digits[:i+1] + digits[i+1:][::-1]\n\nBut this would create new lists each time, avoiding in-place modifications.\n\nThis approach avoids modifying the original list in place, thus avoiding side effects. That's better according to the rules.\n\nSo, let's adjust the code accordingly.\n\nNow, putting it all together:\n\ndef rearrange_bigger(n):\n    digits = list(str(n))\n    i = len(digits) - 2\n    while i >= 0 and digits[i] >= digits[i+1]:\n        i -= 1\n    if i == -1:\n        return False\n    j = len(digits) - 1\n    while digits[j] <= digits[i]:\n        j -= 1\n    # create a new list with digits[i] and digits[j] swapped\n    digits = digits[:i] + [digits[j]] + digits[i+1:j] + [digits[i]] + digits[j+1:]\n    # reverse the suffix after i\n    digits = digits[:i+1] + digits[i+1: